Subject: [PATCH] player: hand vo_mediacodec a frame without waiting for the next one

The core queues a video frame only once the frame after it is decoded, so it
can compute the first one's duration. vo_mediacodec times every release from
the frame's own pts and needs neither that duration nor the next frame first,
but the wait still applies to it. On an Amlogic S928X box playing a 1080i50
recording at 25 Hz, the decoder emits each frame only about 110 ms before its
deadline, so the next frame usually arrives about 70 ms before the current
one's deadline, and whenever the decoder ran later than that the current
frame reached the codec after it: 0.3-0.6 late frames a second, a frame held
a refresh too long each time. In 111 of 116 such frames, the frame itself had
been decoded and its VO slot freed a median 124 ms before its deadline;
ExoPlayer, which releases each frame on its own timestamp, kept every frame on
the same decoder and mode (Plezy #2485).

VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING declares that a VO times frames itself; vo_mediacodec sets
it. For such a VO, get_queue_frames() lets one decoded frame go to the VO,
while needs_new_frame() still has the decoder run get_req_frames() ahead, and
video_output_image() keeps asking for that next frame in the pass that finds
a frame already queueable, so the decoder is asked as early as before. A frame
queued alone takes the spacing from the previous frame's pts as its duration
(the stream's own cadence, where the container rate would be half that of a
decoder splitting fields into frames), and that spacing also settles the
previous frame's duration, which may have been such an estimate. A spacing of
zero (a repeated timestamp) or of more than twice the previous estimate (a
timestamp jump, which belongs to the previous frame) keeps the previous
frame's estimate. The first frame after a reset, display-sync and every other
VO keep the lookahead.

diff --git a/player/video.c b/player/video.c
index 0e1ac29..6f9aec7 100644
--- a/player/video.c
+++ b/player/video.c
@@ -453,11 +453,34 @@ static void add_new_frame(struct MPContext *mpctx, struct mp_image *frame)
         handle_new_frame(mpctx);
 }
 
+// Frames needed in mpctx->next_frames[] before the first may go to the VO.
+// Normally all get_req_frames() asks for, the second giving the first its
+// duration. A VO that times each frame from its own pts (VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING)
+// takes one as soon as it is decoded: waiting for the next one only delays
+// it, by however late the decoder is with that next one, and a MediaCodec
+// decoder that emits frames a little ahead of their deadline makes that the
+// difference between on time and late. calculate_frame_duration() then uses
+// the spacing just measured. Decoding still runs get_req_frames() ahead
+// (needs_new_frame), so the decoder is asked for the next frame as early as
+// before. The first frame after a reset (nothing measured yet) and
+// display-sync, which schedules vsyncs from each frame's duration, keep the
+// lookahead.
+static int get_queue_frames(struct MPContext *mpctx, bool eof)
+{
+    int req = get_req_frames(mpctx, eof);
+    if (req > 1 && (mpctx->video_out->driver->caps & VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING) &&
+        mpctx->video_pts != MP_NOPTS_VALUE && mpctx->num_past_frames >= 1 &&
+        !mpctx->display_sync_active &&
+        mpctx->video_out->opts->video_sync == VS_DEFAULT)
+        return 1;
+    return req;
+}
+
 // Enough video filtered already to push one frame to the VO?
 // Set eof to true if no new frames are to be expected.
 static bool have_new_frame(struct MPContext *mpctx, bool eof)
 {
-    return mpctx->num_next_frames >= get_req_frames(mpctx, eof);
+    return mpctx->num_next_frames >= get_queue_frames(mpctx, eof);
 }
 
 // Fill mpctx->next_frames[] with a newly filtered or decoded image.
@@ -487,7 +510,9 @@ static int video_output_image(struct MPContext *mpctx, bool *logical_eof)
         hrseek = false;
     }
 
-    if (have_new_frame(mpctx, false))
+    // A frame may already be queueable while decoding runs a frame further
+    // ahead (get_queue_frames): keep asking for that one in the same pass.
+    if (have_new_frame(mpctx, false) && !needs_new_frame(mpctx))
         return VD_NEW_FRAME;
 
     // Get a new frame if we need one.
@@ -990,6 +1015,37 @@ static void calculate_frame_duration(struct MPContext *mpctx)
     // rounding error into the opposite direction.
     double tolerance = 0.001 * 3 + 0.0001;
 
+    // A VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING VO gets a frame before the next one is decoded
+    // (get_queue_frames), so the previous frame's duration may have been a
+    // guess: this frame's pts settles it. Without a next frame, this frame
+    // takes that spacing: the stream's own cadence, which the container rate
+    // is not for a decoder that splits fields into frames (twice the rate).
+    // A spacing more than twice the previous frame's estimate is a jump in
+    // the timestamps (a broadcast recording's discontinuity), which belongs
+    // to the previous frame, not this one; so is a spacing of zero (a decoder
+    // that repeats a timestamp across a field pair). Either keeps the
+    // previous frame's estimate.
+    if ((mpctx->video_out->driver->caps & VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING) &&
+        mpctx->num_past_frames >= 2)
+    {
+        double pts0 = mpctx->past_frames[0].pts;
+        double prev = mpctx->past_frames[1].pts;
+        double prev_estimate = mpctx->past_frames[1].approx_duration;
+        double spacing = pts0 != MP_NOPTS_VALUE && prev != MP_NOPTS_VALUE
+                       ? pts0 - prev : -1;
+        if (spacing > 0)
+            mpctx->past_frames[1].duration = spacing;
+        if (mpctx->num_next_frames < 2) {
+            if (spacing > 0 && (prev_estimate <= 0 ||
+                                spacing <= 2 * prev_estimate + tolerance))
+            {
+                duration = spacing;
+            } else if (prev_estimate > 0) {
+                duration = prev_estimate;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
     double total = 0;
     int num_dur = 0;
     for (int n = 1; n < mpctx->num_past_frames; n++) {
diff --git a/video/out/vo.h b/video/out/vo.h
index 9d340f7..ad48547 100644
--- a/video/out/vo.h
+++ b/video/out/vo.h
@@ -210,6 +210,10 @@ enum {
     VO_CAP_FRAMEOWNER   = 1 << 5,
     // VO does handle mp_image_params.vflip
     VO_CAP_VFLIP        = 1 << 6,
+    // VO times every frame from that frame's own pts, and needs neither the
+    // next frame queued behind it nor the core to wait for one: the core may
+    // hand it a frame before the following one is decoded (vo_mediacodec).
+    VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING   = 1 << 7,
 };
 
 enum {
diff --git a/video/out/vo_mediacodec.c b/video/out/vo_mediacodec.c
index f63d786..da86096 100644
--- a/video/out/vo_mediacodec.c
+++ b/video/out/vo_mediacodec.c
@@ -3405,7 +3405,12 @@ const struct vo_driver video_out_mediacodec = {
     // release call the drop buys no throughput and only empties the
     // screen. A burst of late frames still collapses at the compositor,
     // which shows the newest queued buffer per vsync.
-    .caps = VO_CAP_FRAMEDROP,
+    //
+    // Every release is timed from the frame's own pts (get_release_target),
+    // so the frame after it is not needed first; OWN_TIMING lets the core
+    // hand a frame over as soon as it is decoded instead of once the next
+    // one is (player/video.c get_queue_frames).
+    .caps = VO_CAP_FRAMEDROP | VO_CAP_OWN_TIMING,
     .preinit = preinit,
     .query_format = query_format,
     .prepare_frame = prepare_frame,
